[ ] Move the sandbox image to a root Containerfile =sandbox-root-containerfile $podman build finds a root Containerfile with no flags, so the convention is simply the repo's default Containerfile; any repo that has one works with the sandbox. Move agents/Containerfile to the platform root and add a root .containerignore holding a single asterisk: code arrives through the mount, and without it every build ships clearhead-core/target and node_modules as context. One environment for the superproject; per-submodule images plus compose were rejected as ceremony, since scoping a run to one submodule needs only the prompt.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T15:32 #01a0dab3-2d6b-7c92-9bef-3457a5bf4c4e
[ ] Make the queue loop an orchestrator over work runs =sandbox-driver-split $DECIDED 2026-09-25: orchestration is policy and sits outside the run primitives. Today it is done by the human, or by an interactive agent reading run manifests; the queue loop becomes one more orchestrator, for unattended nights, that starts work runs, not part of the sandbox. Dependency direction: the sandbox runner knows git, podman and harnesses, never ClearHead; the loop, its prompt template and its completed, blocked or unfinished classification live on the ClearHead side and call the runner. The runner's manifest records exit, cost, closing message and commits, never actions. Do after sandbox-root-containerfile and alongside the review-run work.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T15:32 #01a0dab3-2d95-7d53-a65e-cb88d090e1bd
[ ] Snapshot the harness into each run =sandbox-harness-snapshot $agents/ is mounted live into the running container, so editing agents/loop mid-run can corrupt a shell that reads it incrementally. agent-run copies the harness into the run directory and mounts that copy read-only: each run keeps exactly the harness it started with, which also makes runs reproducible.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T15:32 #01a0dab3-2db5-76e3-b433-81d7e9963547
[ ] Export sandbox telemetry with standard attributes =sandbox-telemetry $Sessions export OTLP to the NUC collector, which writes agent-logs, agent-metrics and agent-traces jsonl under ~/.local/state/otelcol. From a container, the collector answers on the tailscale address, not host.containers.internal. Attributes, standard only: service.name and service.version from the harness itself, host.name, deployment.environment.name sandbox or interactive, vcs.repository.name, vcs.ref.head.name, vcs.ref.head.revision, cicd.pipeline.name agent-sandbox, cicd.pipeline.run.id for the run and cicd.pipeline.task.run.id per session. No clearhead attributes: runs join to actions through commits. The driver also timestamps build and each session in the manifest. Write the convention, with this table and its reasons, to docs/telemetry.md; interactive harness configs adopt host.name and deployment.environment.name.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T15:32 #01a0dab3-2dd5-7753-9f1d-25c1a3aea18a
[ ] Query sandbox runs with duckdb =sandbox-benchmarks $A saved duckdb query file over ~/agent-runs manifests and the collector jsonl, answering: cost and time per outcome; build time against agent time; tool calls and gate runs per session; sandbox against interactive sessions. Questions first, no dashboard. Do after sandbox-telemetry.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T15:32 #01a0dab3-2df6-7862-8b3a-df005de98823
[ ] Add review runs and harness selection =sandbox-cross-vendor-review $DECIDED 2026-09-25 with the human: work and review are separate run kinds, never bundled; deciding which to run is orchestration, kept outside both. A work run takes an action or prompt and mounts its clone read-write; its output is commits and a closing message. A review run takes a finished run's branch, refs/agent/base to agent/id, mounts that clone read-only so report-without-fixing is enforced, and writes findings to its manifest; no commits. Every run picks its harness, claude or pi, through an adapter taking a prompt file and a transcript path and reporting result and cost; a review usually uses the other vendor. The work prompt drops its self-review step, and the runbook's review gate moves to between a work run and landing. pi: headless via pi -p --mode json, final answer from the last message_end event, cost summed from usage.cost; image pins pi 0.85.1; the sandbox's own ChatGPT/Codex login lives in the agent-pi volume, OpenRouter as fallback via the openrouter_token secret; the subscription quota is shared with interactive work. agent-run shares the host pi settings minus pi-background-tasks, remote-pi and pi-memory, and the host agents directory, read-only.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T15:32 #01a0dab3-2e1a-79c2-a788-6901734ddadf
[ ] Extract the sandbox into its own repo =sandbox-extract $Decided 2026-09-25 after evaluate-agent-runners: we write our own. Do after sandbox-driver-split, once the runner, harness adapters, harvest and land contain no ClearHead reference; grep for it as the check. The ClearHead driver stays on the ClearHead side. Extraction surfaces the remaining platform assumptions: the agents directory mount, the host pi paths, agent branch naming, and harvest assuming submodules. Proof of done: a run, harvest and land on a second repo that has no .clearhead directory.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T16:13 #01a0dad8-d9ba-79f1-8a9f-b5b68c0e3b0a
[ ] Summarize running and finished runs on demand =sandbox-status $scripts/agent-status, for all runs or one id: state, elapsed, idle seconds since the last transcript event, event count, and the last few tool calls. Stuck means the container is running and idle for more than N minutes; the transcript's modification time is the heartbeat, so nothing hooks into the harness. Each harness adapter owns summarizing its own event stream, since claude stream-json and pi json mode differ; the status command stays generic. This is the on-demand bridge: the lasting answer is telemetry, sandbox-telemetry, which serves the same questions from the collector across sandbox and interactive sessions and every exporting harness. Found 2026-09-25: the only live signal was podman ps, while the transcript already held the whole story.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T16:26 #01a0dae3-f79f-7601-8d06-00c5b01ae657
